<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simon de la Loubère</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1642">1642</a>–<a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as La Loubère</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simon de la Loubère was a French diplomat, writer, mathematician and poet who led an embassy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> of Siam in 1687. The mission, of five warships, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in October 1687, and he sailed home in January 1688 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Guy Tachard and a Siamese embassy. At the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> he described the country in Du Royaume de Siam (1691), which included the astronomer Jean Dominique Cassini's reading of a Siamese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of Indian origin, the first foundation of European scholarship on Indian astronomy. He also brought back the 'Siamese method' of making magic squares, and sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> from 1693.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-de-la-loubere.jpg" alt="Simon de la Loubère" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simon_de_La_Loubere_Portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pali was first mentioned in Western writing in <b>Simon de la Loubère</b>’s account of his travels in Siam.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Masters set their slaves to work on their lands and gardens and in their households, or let them earn their own living in return for a yearly payment: <b>Simon de la Loubère</b>, who visited in 1687, put it at four to eight ticals.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Montesquieu-Volvestre, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=1698" aria-label="Toulouse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4585 618 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Simon de la Loubère"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=1698">What was here in 1698? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
